 "title": "Have You Outgrown Your Customers?",
 "topic": "sales",
 "summary": "Established business owners often find their businesses evolve faster than their target audience. The creator claims that businesses end up marketing to the same old audience, leading to justified price resistance, while new higher-paying prospects exist.",
 "key_points": [
  "Established business owners' companies often evolve faster than the customers they attract.",
  "People who were wonderful customers five years ago may no longer be the right target.",
  "Marketing to the same audience forces businesses to justify higher prices to people who cannot appreciate the value.",
  "There may be an entirely different group of prospective customers willing to pay considerably more.",
  "Exercise: Describe your ideal customer today without considering who has historically purchased from you.",
  "Determine what your ideal customer would be willing to spend and what they would appreciate most.",
  "Determine where you need to establish your visibility to attract those new customers.",
  "You may not need to change your business; you may just need to change whom you are marketing to."
 ],
 "content": "Have you outgrown your customers? Something I’ve noticed when working with established business owners is that sometimes, their businesses evolve much faster than the customers that they’re attracting. In other words, the people who were wonderful customers five years ago are no longer the people that they should be targeting and this is actually surprisingly common. They’re still marketing to essentially the same audience and as a result, they often find themselves having to justify their prices to people who simply aren’t in a position to appreciate the value of what they now offer. Now, meanwhile, there maybe an entirely different group of prospective customers who would happily pay considerably more. So, here’s a little exercise that I’d encourage you to do. Describe your ideal customer today without taking into consideration who’s historically purchased from you. What would they be ideally willing to spend? What would they particularly appreciate about what you provide now and where would you need to establish your visibility in order to attract those people? What you may discover is that your what you offer is spot on, but you may need to change who you’re marketing to. Of course, identifying that new audience is easier when someone can look at your business from the outside, which is something that we can do together in a private strategy session. I’ll work with you to identify your next caliber of customer and figure out where to reach them. So, if that’s of interest, go ahead and comment coach and I’ll send you the information. This is Mark Satterfield. Thanks so much for watching. Please let and subscribe if you haven’t already and of course, I always appreciate you passing these videos along to others who are marketing their products or services to the affluent. I’ll talk to you soon. Bye for now. Text on screen: Have you outgrown your customers?",
